"w.desert.t": "Desert — Yellow",
"w.desert.g": "He walks with the uncertain step of someone who thinks they know where they're going. The lasso — not a whole one, just enough to believe in it — hangs from his hand like a hope too light for this heat. Yellow in the desert: naivety confronting the infinite without knowing it. Jeans, cowboy hat, the gear of conquest worn by someone who has probably never conquered anything. And yet he walks.",
"w.desert.s": "Naivety &amp; Terra Incognita",
"w.desert.cs": "He has no idea where he's going. He still brought a hat.",
"w.desert.situ": "Desert — Yellow, in situ",
"w.forest.t": "Forest — Blue",
"w.forest.g": "He's running toward us. But has he just been found — or is he fleeing something? After months wandering through the forest, adventurer's hat fixed on his head and battered backpack in tow, Blue bursts from the vegetation with a survivor's energy. The work poses the fundamental question of all wandering: at what point does a lost path become the right path?",
"w.forest.s": "Wandering &amp; Resilience",
"w.forest.cs": "Lost, but in good shape.",
"w.forest.situ": "Forest — Blue, in situ",
"w.city.t": "City — Red",
"w.city.g": "He doesn't doubt. That's what makes him fascinating — and slightly insufferable. Red in the city moves through the skyscrapers with the quiet certainty of someone who feels at home everywhere: perfect bowler hat, briefcase in hand, small city shoes clicking on the asphalt. The work interrogates confidence as a social construction. Or perhaps just as a temperament.",
"w.city.s": "Hubris &amp; Asphalt",
"w.city.cs": "He thinks he's at home everywhere. He's wrong, with great confidence.",
"w.city.situ": "City — Red, in situ",
"w.mountain.t": "Mountain — Green",
"w.mountain.g": "She jumps. That's all. In the immaculate snow, under an insultingly clear sky, Green jumps — sunglasses on her forehead, smile on her lips, no calculation, no pose. The conditions are idyllic, she knows it, and she enjoys them without apology. In a world that performs constantly, pure carefreeness may be the most radical posture there is.",
"w.mountain.s": "Carefree &amp; Altitude",
"w.mountain.cs": "She doesn't ask anyone's permission. I envy her.",
"w.mountain.situ": "Mountain — Green, in situ",
"w.ocean.t": "Ocean — Orange",
"w.ocean.g": "Something is there, in the depths. Orange knows it. His flippers, his scuba tank, all his equipment are no help against what he cannot name. Shark? Shadow? The mysteries of the ocean cannot be catalogued. The work explores that borderline where preparation gives way to dread. You can be perfectly equipped and still be afraid.",
"w.ocean.s": "Abyss &amp; Dread",
"w.ocean.cs": "All the gear in the world, and still that face.",
"w.ocean.situ": "Ocean — Orange, in situ",
"w.air.t": "Air — Orange",
"w.air.g": "<em>Vayu</em>, the vital breath, the <em>prana</em> that circulates through all things; <em>Fū</em>, the Japanese element of free movement, of the spirit that cannot be grasped. Orange embodies this duality in its most raw expression. The composition is stripped bare: a sky, a plane receding into the distance, a blinding sun. Orange is falling. He has a parachute, certainly — but the plane is already too far for him to count on it. His gaze, that unique blend of dread and resignation, captures the suspended instant in which <em>Vayu</em> ceases to be philosophical abstraction and becomes a concrete abyss beneath one's feet. It is a leap into the void. It is every leap into the void.",
"w.air.s": "Vayu · Fū",
"w.air.cs": "The plane has left. It often does.",
"w.air.situ": "Air — Orange, in situ",
"w.earth.t": "Earth — Red",
"w.earth.g": "<em>Prithvi</em> is the Earth goddess, the support of all existence; <em>Chi</em>, in the Godai, embodies solidity, resistance, that which endures. Helmet fixed on skull, lamp raised like a relic, Red advances through the cave with the quiet assurance of the discoverer. But the statue that looms over him, carved by forgotten hands, reminds him of the obvious: he is not the first. Even in the very bowels of <em>Prithvi</em>, humanity has preceded him. The work interrogates this vertiginous paradox inherent to the Earth element — those who believe they explore most often only rediscover. The Earth keeps everything. The Earth remembers.",
"w.earth.s": "Prithvi · Chi",
"w.earth.cs": "He thought he was the first. Nobody is.",
"w.earth.situ": "Earth — Red, in situ",
"w.fire.t": "Fire — Blue",
"w.fire.g": "In Hindu cosmogony, <em>Agni</em> is the sacrificial fire, the force that transforms matter into energy; in Japanese tradition, <em>Ka</em> embodies passion, movement, and will. Blue belongs to this dual lineage. On his incandescent promontory, the bucket he carries is no mere container: it is the Promethean gesture by which humanity wrenched from the volcano its most burning secret. Behind him, the eruption rumbles — relic of an era when <em>Agni</em> reigned unchallenged. Today, Blue carries it. Domesticated. Tamed. Mundane. The force that once devoured civilisations now serves to boil water for tea.",
"w.fire.s": "Agni · Ka",
"w.fire.cs": "He stole fire from a volcano. For tea.",
"w.fire.situ": "Fire — Blue, in situ",
"w.water.t": "Water — Green",
"w.water.g": "<em>Jala</em>, in Vedic thought, is the primordial matrix, the cosmic amniotic fluid; <em>Sui</em>, in the Japanese Godai, designates that which flows, adapts, and takes the form of its vessel. The sole female figure in the M&amp;M pantheon — troubling coincidence, or symbolic inevitability? — Green rises at the heart of an aquatic sanctuary, arms open in welcome. Water, source of all life, envelops her. The white horse in the background speaks of ancestral purity; its saddle speaks of conquest. The work oscillates between celebration and domestication, between primordial fluidity and human mastery. The rainbow is not décor: it is the covenant, in both the biblical and Vedic sense.",
"w.water.s": "Jala · Sui",
"w.water.cs": "The horse wasn't planned. It stayed.",
"w.water.situ": "Water — Green, in situ",
"w.space.t": "Space — Yellow",
"w.space.g": "<em>Akasha</em>, the ether, the fifth element, the one that contains all others; <em>Kū</em>, the Japanese void, not absence but pure potentiality. Yellow floats in this metaphysical between-space. Above a blue-tinged Earth, he waves with candid gesture at an immensity that sovereignly ignores him. The Moon, disproportionate, occupies nearly as much space as the distant Sun — a masterful optical illusion that interrogates our relationship to proximity. What appears close rarely is. What appears distant governs us. A satellite crosses the frame, a discreet reminder that even <em>Akasha</em>, once pristine, now bears our technological fingerprints.",
"w.space.s": "Akasha · Kū",
"w.space.cs": "Same smile as in the desert. He learned nothing, and that's fine.",
"w.space.situ": "Space — Yellow, in situ",
