"works.lead": "Two series of five oil paintings, each preceded by dozens of pencil sketches, released as limited-edition prints on aluminium.",
"corr.desc": "Each M&amp;M appears in two series — an Environment, an Element. Far from incidental, this doubling composes a secret grammar: it is in the passage from one frame to the other, in the silent echo between the two faces of the same character, that the true meaning of the work reveals itself. Five colours, five diptychs, five vertigos.",
"corr.jaune.title": "Yellow — From Desert to Space",
"corr.jaune.sub": "Desert · Space · Two absolute silences",
"corr.jaune.desc": "Two deserts. This is the vertiginous crossing the Yellow proposes by juxtaposing its two appearances. In the <em>Environments</em> series, it crosses an arid wasteland — cracked earth, cacti standing like silent exclamation marks, an unrelenting sun. In <em>Elements</em>, it floats in the stellar void. Geographically, everything separates them. Metaphysically, nothing. These are two expanses where life refuses to flourish except at its most extreme, two absolute silences, two fundamental hostilities. Dry rock and void share this same ontological quality: they are what remains when everything else has given up. The Yellow's candid smile, identical in both works, takes on the value of a manifesto — ignorance as the last survival strategy in the face of the uninhabitable.",
"corr.rouge.title": "Red — From the City to Lost Civilizations",
"corr.rouge.sub": "City · Earth · Millennia in a single glance",
"corr.rouge.desc": "The Red proposes here the longest temporal crossing of the series. On one side, the contemporary metropolis, its neon lights, its din. On the other, the cave and its pre-Columbian statue, silent witness to an engulfed world. Between the two, millennia — and yet, the same human obstinacy to leave traces. Today's skyscrapers are merely tomorrow's pyramids; yesterday's statues were the selfies of their time. The work questions this troubling continuity: everything we build is already a ruin in potential. The Red, indifferent, traverses the ages with the same quiet assurance — as if it knew that civilisations pass but the need to civilise endures.",
"corr.bleu.title": "Blue — From Forest to Volcano",
"corr.bleu.sub": "Forest · Fire · The breath of Brahma",
"corr.bleu.desc": "Forest and fire. Creation and destruction. The Blue embodies this cosmic pulse that the Hindus make the very breath of Brahma. In the forest environment, it moves among the trees — biomass, oxygen, photosynthesis, the secret factory of the living. In the volcanic environment, it carries the bucket of lava, that same living reduced to ash. Forest and fire are not opposites: they are the two phases of the same movement. Every forest will eventually burn. Every burned earth will eventually turn green again. The Blue, moving from one to the other, is not a character changing scenery: it is the witness of the cycle.",
"corr.vert.title": "Green — From Mountains to Water",
"corr.vert.sub": "Mountain · Water · Two faces of the same journey",
"corr.vert.desc": "Water everywhere. This is the stubborn message of the Green. In the mountains, it sleeps as ice, runs in torrents, seeps from rock faces. In the <em>Elements</em> series, it opens onto a lake — generous, welcoming, a mirror of the sky. From the most austere altitude to the most peaceful valley, it is the same substance circulating, the same energy transformed by the journey. The white horse that appears in this aquatic sanctuary speaks to that continuity: a creature of movement, it bridges the severity of the peaks and the serenity of the lake, as if the Green's easy confidence on its mountain had simply found, below, its calmer reflection.",
"corr.orange.title": "Orange — From Sea to Air",
"corr.orange.sub": "Ocean · Air · Two conquests, one fragility",
"corr.orange.desc": "Two conquests. This is what the Orange celebrates — and questions. The sea and the air are, on this planet, the two environments that humans could not inhabit; they are also the two they eventually crossed. The diving suit and the aeroplane are the improbable twins of the same Promethean audacity. Yet the Orange never triumphs. In the maritime environment, it dives into the abyss — a fragile coloured dot facing whatever lurks in the depths. In the aerial element, it falls — a coloured dot on the void. The same fragility, the same hostile blue, the same fundamental precariousness. The Orange reminds us that to conquer is not to inhabit, and that every victory over the elements remains, in the final instance, a fragile truce.",
"bio.p1": "Candice Shell was born in Ohio in 1978. Her father was a food flavour chemist. She grew up among vials labelled \"strawberry flavour\" and \"vanilla flavour\", convinced that everything edible was first invented in a lab.",
"bio.p2": "For fifteen years she was an art director at a Chicago agency specialising in candy and cereal packaging. She reckons she drew some 40,000 smiles there. None of them, she says, was ever allowed to hesitate.",
"bio.p3": "In 2019, a client asked her for the fourth time to make a mascot \"happier\". She stood up, walked out of the meeting and never came back. Five years followed without her drawing a single smile.",
"bio.p4": "In 2024 she drove across Arizona with no particular destination. She came back with the idea for her first work: Yellow, lost in the desert, cowboy hat on his head, a lasso too short in his hand. She later admitted it was a self-portrait.",
"bio.p5": "Her first series, <i>Environments</i> (2025), throws five mascots into worlds too big for them: desert, forest, city, mountain, ocean. For the first time, characters condemned to smile are allowed to be afraid, to doubt, to get lost.",
"bio.p6": "In 2026, a stay in Kyoto and then Rishikesh led her to the Japanese Godai and the Vedic Pañca Bhūta. The result is <i>Elements</i>, her second series, in which each colour joins a primordial element. She describes the trip herself as \"a former American ad exec's spiritual crisis, clichés included\".",
"bio.p7": "She now lives in Madrid, in the Lavapiés neighbourhood. She paints in oil on canvas, after dozens of pencil sketches of each character, a habit left over from the mascot model sheets of her old job. Her canvases are signed on the back, then released as limited-edition prints on aluminium. She is represented exclusively by Tonton Gug Studio. People who meet her are surprised: she smiles a lot. \"They're the only smiles nobody ordered from me.\"",
"tl.1": "Stay in Kyoto, then Rishikesh; Series II, <i>Elements</i>; moves to Madrid",
"tl.2": "Series I, <i>Environments</i>, opened by Yellow in the desert",
"tl.3": "Drives across Arizona with no destination",
"tl.4": "Five years without drawing a single smile",
